+++ b/docs/transport-upgrade.md
@@ -0,0 +1,73 @@
+# Collector transport upgrade
+
+2026-10-03. The root manifest now pins Primus 8.0.9 and ws 8.22.0. Their locked
+dependency changes replace the affected ws 1.1.5 parser and Primus 6.1.0's old
+setheader dependency. The collector, writer, API and browser application source
+remain unchanged. Both existing Primus plugins retain their versions.
+
+The package archives matched npm's published SHA-512 integrity values before
+inspection. Primus 8's WebSocket adapter uses the upgrade request directly and
+converts text frames to strings for the existing JSON parser. It supports the
+current ws interface while retaining the efsn `emit` envelopes and Primus
+ping/pong protocol. There is no efsn source, mining or consensus change here.
+
+Only the root package/lock pair changes. The v1 lock retains other direct
+dependency versions; 17 package-path records change, including removals and
+relocations. The total goes from 331 to 333 entries. A clean backend installation
+with npm 10.9.0 and scripts disabled leaves the lock intact; all 333 installed
+versions match it. Frontend and separate API locks remain unchanged.
+
+The repeated npm audit no longer flags ws, Primus or setheader. Root findings
+fall from 44 to 41: 12 critical, 18 high, six moderate and five low. Those remaining
+entries still need the [runtime/dependency worklist](runtime-dependency-review.md).
+The unchanged legacy `http2` engine warning and other dependency deprecations
+remain recorded. This update is not approval of the complete runtime dependency
+tree or public hosting.
+
+## Boundary and parser settings
+
+The underlying byte maximum is now inclusive. A configured limit of 2048 accepts
+2048 bytes and rejects 2049; the older parser rejected 2048 too. The first socket
+run retained that old expectation and timed out waiting for a valid connection
+to close. Its evidence is retained. The updated test explicitly checks the new
+boundary, including successful delivery at the maximum, plus the existing
+multibyte and aggregate-fragment cases. No production limit values were raised.
+
+One added test reads the actual WebSocket server options on all three collector
+paths. It confirms the configured byte cap, default 16,384 message-fragment and
+262,144 buffered-chunk caps, disabled compression and enabled UTF-8 validation.
+The test uses ordinary startup and configuration inspection. It does not attempt
+to exhaust memory or replay advisory exploit examples. These parser count caps
+do not establish a bound on total process memory.
+
+See [input limits](collector-input-limits.md) for the current configuration
+contract. Finite output queues, credential checks and telemetry admission rules
+continue to apply separately.
+
+## Local acceptance
+
+Linux Node 24.21.0 passes all 173 contract and 14 socket tests. The actual efsn
+WSS/PostgreSQL/HTTPS/browser fixture passes direct RPC comparisons, fifty-block
+history and four browser checkpoints including writer expiry/recovery. The
+eight-reporter proxy scenario passes unchanged, including normalized forwarding
+headers, private routes, connection limits and a 3,276,956-byte history reply.
+
+The collector-loss mining drill also passes: the isolated synthetic chain mines
+twelve blocks and makes twelve automatic ticket purchases while its collector
+is stopped. Collector restoration requires no node or writer restart. Height 74
+reaches HTTPS, all thirteen purchases since height 61 match canonical receipts,
+and the recovered chart covers heights 35–74. P17's telemetry fix still yields
+heads 73, 73, 74, 74 after reconnect. Real backup data and real signing keys were
+not used. Test services stop and temporary credentials are removed.
+
+The original 547-file compiled frontend remains unchanged. These local results
+do not select a public host or approve the remaining dependency tree.
+
+## Remaining work
+
+Continue the dependency review with active Lodash/Express/GeoIP and unused legacy
+tools, the separate API install, browser Axios and the old deployment workflow.
+Then freeze and verify the final fresh-install release matrix and complete
+supervision, public certificates, retries/log lifecycle and hosting acceptance.
+The efsn investigation retains exact locks, package integrity, audit output and
+test captures under `docs/evidence/restart-dashboard-transport-2026-10-03/`.
